var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Low-Level-/-Expert-API","page":"Low-Level API","text":"This page documents the older low-level stepping surface built around SimulationState, build_simulation, and explicit integrator specs.\n\nUse this layer when you need direct control over GPU-resident arrays, manual stepping, or debugging of execution details. Normal scripts should prefer the workflow API shown in the Quickstart.","title":"Low-Level / Expert API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#1)-Install-and-verify-CUDA","page":"Low-Level API","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"<repository-url>\")  # or local dev path\n\nusing CUDA\nCUDA.functional() || error(\"CUDA is not functional on this machine.\")\n\nParticleDynamics is GPU-only: simulation buffers stay GPU-resident, and the current implementation uses CUDA-backed arrays internally.","title":"1) Install and verify CUDA"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#2)-First-low-level-simulation-(2D,-tiny-and-fast)","page":"Low-Level API","text":"using ParticleDynamics: build_simulation, step!, nve, velocityverlet, baoab, eulerheun, eulermaruyama,\n    write_xyz!, write_observables_csv!, gsd_open, gsd_close, write_gsd_frame!\nusing CUDA\n\nN = 64\ndt = 2.0f-4\nsigma = 1.0f0\nrcut = Float32(2^(1/6)) * sigma  # WCA cutoff\n\nst = build_simulation(\n    N=N,\n    box=(40.0f0, 40.0f0),\n    cutoff=rcut,\n    skin=0.4f0,\n    cap=Int32(32),\n    neigh_interval=10,\n    epsilon=10.0f0,\n    sigma=sigma,\n    gamma=50.0f0,\n    temperature=1.0f0,\n    nonbonded=:wca,\n    precision=:f32,\n)\n\n# Preferred stepping path: keep an explicit integrator spec and reuse it.\nvv = velocityverlet(st; gamma=50.0f0, temperature=1.0f0, dt=dt)\nfor _ in 1:200\n    step!(st, vv, dt; compute_energy=false)\nend\n\n@show st.step\n\nExpected result: st.step == 200 and finite state arrays.","title":"2) First low-level simulation (2D, tiny and fast)"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#3)-Explicit-integrator-selection","page":"Low-Level API","text":"# Deterministic NVE velocity Verlet\nnve_spec = nve(st; dt=dt)\nstep!(st, nve_spec, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\n# Langevin BAOAB\nbao = baoab(st; gamma=50.0f0, temperature=1.0f0, dt=dt)\nstep!(st, bao, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\n# Brownian Euler-Maruyama\nem = eulermaruyama(st; gamma=50.0f0, temperature=1.0f0, dt=dt)\nstep!(st, em, dt; compute_energy=false)\n\n# Brownian midpoint (Euler-Heun)\neh = eulerheun(st; gamma=50.0f0, temperature=1.0f0, dt=dt)\nstep!(st, eh, dt; compute_energy=false)\n\nExplicit specs are the stepping API. Stochastic parameters such as gamma, temperature, and OU correlation time belong to the integrator constructor, not to SimulationState.","title":"3) Explicit integrator selection"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Important-runtime-policy","page":"Low-Level API","text":"For stochastic paths that divide by friction, gamma must be strictly positive.   Tests enforce informative errors for invalid gamma <= 0 usage on BAOAB/Brownian/EM paths.   Use nve(st; dt=...) for deterministic inertial dynamics instead of encoding NVE through zero-friction stochastic parameters.","title":"Important runtime policy"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#4)-Simple-output-writing","page":"Low-Level API","text":"write_xyz!(\"traj.xyz\", st, st.step)\nwrite_observables_csv!(\"obs.csv\", st.step; Epot=st.Epot, Ekin=st.Ekin, dq=st.dq)\n\ngsdh = gsd_open(\"traj.gsd\")\nwrite_gsd_frame!(gsdh, st; diameter=1.0f0, types_names=[\"A\"], step=st.step)\ngsd_close(gsdh)","title":"4) Simple output writing"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#5)-Reproducibility-basics","page":"Low-Level API","text":"using Random, CUDA\nRandom.seed!(0xBADC0DE)\nCUDA.seed!(UInt64(0xBADC0DE))\n\nThis gives reproducible runs at the statistical/moment level.   Bitwise-identical trajectories across different GPUs/toolchains are not guaranteed.\n\nAdvanced orchestration helpers that are not part of the default import surface remain available under qualified paths such as ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.","title":"5) Reproducibility basics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#6)-Where-to-copy-realistic-parameter-sets","page":"Low-Level API","text":"Use repository examples as templates:\n\nexamples/2D_example.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_BAOAB.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_BD_EH.jl\nexamples/2D_active_OU_brownian.jl\nexamples/3D_BD.jl\nexamples/3D_LJ_NVE.jl\n\nThese scripts contain production-like parameter scales; for quick iteration/tests, downscale N and total steps first.","title":"6) Where to copy realistic parameter sets"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Forces","page":"Forces","text":"ParticleDynamics exposes high-level parameter and topology objects for bonded and nonbonded interactions. Force kernels themselves remain internal and CUDA-specific.","title":"Forces"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Public-parameter-and-bond-API","page":"Forces","text":"","title":"Public parameter and bond API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Current-interaction-families","page":"Forces","text":"nonbonded Lennard-Jones\nnonbonded WCA\nnonbonded soft-repulsive\nharmonic bonds\nFENE bonds\n\nThe stable user surface is the parameter/topology layer plus build_simulation(...; nonbonded=..., bonding=..., bonds=...).","title":"Current interaction families"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Example-scripts","page":"Forces","text":"examples/2D_soft_repulsive.jl: soft-repulsive neighbor-list path\nexamples/3D_stencil_two_sizes.jl: mixed-size cutoff workflow\nbonded polymer examples under examples/ remain GPU-first today and should only be treated as future CPU-compatible candidates after a real CPU backend lands","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/forces/#Related-pages","page":"Forces","text":"Simulation State\nObservables","title":"Related pages"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.LJParams","page":"Forces","text":"LJParams{T}(ϵ, σ, rcut)\n\nLennard-Jones parameters used by the nonbonded force kernels.\n\nArguments\n\nϵ: Depth of the LJ well (energy scale).\nσ: Particle diameter (sets the zero of the LJ potential).\nrcut: Pairwise cutoff distance. For the WCA branch this is set to 2^(1/6)σ as in examples/2D_allpairs_quicktest.jl and the 3D scripts.\n\nExamples\n\nparams = LJParams(Float32(10), Float32(1), Float32(2^(1/6)))\nst = build_simulation(N=256, box=(80f0, 80f0), cutoff=params.rcut,\n                      skin=0.4f0, cap=Int32(64),\n                      neigh_interval=10, use_neighborlist=false,\n                      epsilon=params.ϵ, sigma=params.σ,\n                      gamma=50f0, temperature=1f0,\n                      nonbonded=:wca, precision=:f32)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.LJParams"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.SoftRepulsiveParams","page":"Forces","text":"SoftRepulsiveParams{T}(ϵ, σ)\n\nParameters for the truncated harmonic repulsion used in the two-temperature soft-repulsive scripts (e.g. examples/TwoT_2D_LD_VV.jl with σ = 1.0, ϵ = 1e9). The cutoff equals σ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.SoftRepulsiveParams"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.HarmonicBondParams","page":"Forces","text":"HarmonicBondParams{T}(k, r0)\n\nParameters for harmonic bonds U(r) = ½ k (r - r₀)². The polymer examples (examples/2D_polymer_bonded*.jl) use k = 300 and r0 = 1.0 to keep bead spacings near a single diameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.HarmonicBondParams"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.FENEParams","page":"Forces","text":"FENEParams{T}(k, R0)\n\nFinite-extensible nonlinear elastic parameters, with potential U(r) = -½kR₀² log(1 - (r/R₀)²). examples/2D_polymer_bonded_BP.jl demonstrates k = 300, R0 = 1.5 to prevent bond overstretching.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.FENEParams"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.BondPotential","page":"Forces","text":"BondPotential\n\nAbstract supertype for bonded potentials so SimulationState can store either HarmonicBond or FENEBond and dispatch bond kernels accordingly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.BondPotential"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.HarmonicBond","page":"Forces","text":"HarmonicBond(params)\n\nConcrete wrapper storing HarmonicBondParams. Construct via harmonic_bond so the element type matches the simulation precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.HarmonicBond"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.FENEBond","page":"Forces","text":"FENEBond(params)\n\nConcrete wrapper storing FENEParams. Use fene_bond to create instances whose parameter type (Float32/Float64) matches the simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.FENEBond"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.harmonic_bond","page":"Forces","text":"harmonic_bond(; k=100.0, r0=1.0)\n\nConvenience constructor that promotes the supplied spring constant and equilibrium distance to the simulation precision. The polymer demos use harmonic_bond(k=300, r0=1.0) for bead–spring chains.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.harmonic_bond"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.Definitions.fene_bond","page":"Forces","text":"fene_bond(; k=300.0, r0=1.5)\n\nConvenience constructor for FENEBond. The 2D/3D FENE polymer examples draw directly from this helper with the default parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Definitions.fene_bond"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.BondedForces.BondList","page":"Forces","text":"CSR-style adjacency list describing bead connectivity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.BondedForces.BondList"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/forces/#ParticleDynamics.BondedForces.build_bondlist","page":"Forces","text":"build_bondlist(N, bonds) -> BondList\n\nConstruct a GPU-ready bond list from a collection of (i, j) tuples (1-based). examples/2D_polymer_bonded.jl builds its chains via:\n\nchain = collect(zip(1:(n-1), 2:n))\nbond_list = build_bondlist(n, chain)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.BondedForces.build_bondlist"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Observables","page":"Observables","text":"Observables are accumulated on device and exposed through a small public API for step-level diagnostics, virial accumulation, and thermostat exchange tracking.","title":"Observables"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Public-observables-API","page":"Observables","text":"","title":"Public observables API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#What-is-available","page":"Observables","text":"total potential and kinetic energy\nscalar virial and tensor virial\nheat/work accumulators used by stochastic and thermostat paths\nintegrator-specific metadata merged into the returned NamedTuple","title":"What is available"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Collision-counting","page":"Observables","text":"Collision counting is also part of the current top-level API:","title":"Collision counting"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/observables/#Example-scripts","page":"Observables","text":"examples/3D_quicktest.jl: writes energies while stepping\nexamples/ collision-rate workflows: production-style diagnostics, still GPU-first today","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.collect_step_observables","page":"Observables","text":"collect_step_observables(st, spec) -> NamedTuple\n\nCollect universal diagnostics, integrator-specific diagnostics, and bath heat / entropy totals accumulated since the last reset of the relevant buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.collect_step_observables"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.reset_bath_exchange_accumulators!","page":"Observables","text":"reset_bath_exchange_accumulators!(st, spec)\n\nReset the accumulators used for bath heat and entropy diagnostics. This leaves positions, velocities, and thermostat internal state untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.reset_bath_exchange_accumulators!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.accumulate_virial!","page":"Observables","text":"accumulate_virial!(st)\n\nAdd the instantaneous virial trace and total virial tensor into their per-interval accumulators. st.virial remains the scalar trace of the configurational virial tensor for backward compatibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.accumulate_virial!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.virial_components","page":"Observables","text":"virial_components(st)\n\nReturn the component ordering used by the current simulation state's virial tensors.\n\n2D: (:xx, :yy, :xy)\n3D: (:xx, :yy, :zz, :xy, :xz, :yz)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.virial_components"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.virial_tensor","page":"Observables","text":"virial_tensor(st; part=:total, accumulated=false)\n\nReturn the particle-summed configurational virial tensor components as a NamedTuple. The stored sign convention is the raw configurational virial W = Σ r_ij ⊗ F_ij, so pressure/stress formulas may need an additional minus sign depending on convention.\n\nComponent ordering follows:\n\n2D: (:xx, :yy, :xy)\n3D: (:xx, :yy, :zz, :xy, :xz, :yz)\n\nThe primary buffers remain GPU-resident in SimulationState; this helper reduces the selected tensor to a small host-side summary for diagnostics. Virial buffers are refreshed by force evaluations with compute_energy=true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.virial_tensor"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.enable_collision_counting!","page":"Observables","text":"enable_collision_counting!(st; ntypes=nothing, bins=:all_pairs)\n\nAttach GPU buffers that count contact-entry events (pairs whose separation crosses below the collision cutoff). examples/TwoT_2D_LD_VV.jl enables this with ntypes=2 to log cold/cold, cold/hot, and hot/hot encounters.\n\nArguments\n\nntypes: Number of particle types; defaults to maximum(Array(st.typeid)).\nbins: Currently only :all_pairs, which bins every unordered type pair.\n\nExamples\n\nenable_collision_counting!(st; ntypes=2, bins=:all_pairs)\nvv = velocityverlet(st; gamma=gamma, temperature=temperature, dt=dt)\nfor _ in 1:1_000_000\n    step!(st, vv, dt; compute_energy=false)\nend\ncounts = collisions_read_counts!(st) ./ (dt * 1_000_000)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.enable_collision_counting!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.disable_collision_counting!","page":"Observables","text":"disable_collision_counting!(st)\n\nTear down all collision-counting buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.disable_collision_counting!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.collisions_reset_counts!","page":"Observables","text":"collisions_reset_counts!(st)\n\nClear the histogram without reinitializing the contact state. Handy after a warmup period (mirrors the procedure in examples/TwoT_2D_LD_VV.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.collisions_reset_counts!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.collisions_read_counts!","page":"Observables","text":"collisions_read_counts!(st) -> Vector{Int64}\n\nCopy device counters to host. The vector ordering matches the bin assignment printed by enable_collision_counting! (unordered type pairs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.collisions_read_counts!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/observables/#ParticleDynamics.Collisions.set_collision_pair_cutoffs!","page":"Observables","text":"set_collision_pair_cutoffs!(st, rcut_pair)\n\nOverride the contact cutoff used for counting with an ntypes×ntypes matrix. Each entry stores the distance (not squared). This mirrors the RCUT_PAIR setup in examples/3D_stencil_two_sizes.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Collisions.set_collision_pair_cutoffs!"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Groups-and-Filters","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Particle groups are the stable public selection API. The exported Filters module layers higher-level workflows on top of those selections.","title":"Groups and Filters"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Stable-selection-API","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"","title":"Stable selection API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Typical-uses","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"define thermostat baths by particle subset\nassign temperatures or frictions to selected particles\ngather subset observables without copying the whole state to host\nfreeze/tether subsets through the higher-level Filters workflows","title":"Typical uses"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Notes","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"ParticleGroup is the reusable materialized form.\nThe top-level stable API is the group layer above raw implementation buffers.\nThe Filters module remains the place for freeze controls and stochastic selector-based convenience operations.","title":"Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/groups_filters/#Example-scripts","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"examples/TwoT_2D_LD_BAOAB.jl\nexamples/TwoT_SR2D_MD_CSVR_slab.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_freeze_hold.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_freeze_spring.jl\n\nThese examples are GPU-first today. Small subset-control cases should become good CPU-compatible smoke candidates only after a CPU execution backend exists.","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.ParticleSelection","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Abstract base for particle selection specifications.\n\nConcrete subtypes include All, TypeIDs, Indices, and can be extended by users for custom selection logic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.ParticleSelection"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.ParticleGroup","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"ParticleGroup\n\nMaterialized particle selection containing both host and device indices. Reusable across multiple operations without recomputation.\n\nInstances are created via materialize(selection, state).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.ParticleGroup"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.All","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Select all particles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.All"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.TypeIDs","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Select particles by type ID. Accepts a single Int or a vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.TypeIDs"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.Indices","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"Select particles by explicit list of indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.Indices"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.materialize","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"materialize(sel::ParticleSelection, state) -> ParticleGroup\n\nCreate a reusable materialized group from a selection and state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.materialize"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.apply_scalar!","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"apply_scalar!(dest::CuArray, group::ParticleGroup, value)\n\nAssign value to all elements of dest indexed by group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.apply_scalar!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.apply_values!","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"apply_values!(dest::CuArray, group::ParticleGroup, values)\n\nAssign distinct values per group member.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.apply_values!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.gather","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"gather(src::CuArray, group::ParticleGroup) -> Vector\n\nCollect values from device array indexed by group onto the host.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.gather"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/groups_filters/#ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.sum_values","page":"Groups and Filters","text":"sum_values(src::CuArray, group::ParticleGroup) -> Scalar\n\nSum values from device array indexed by group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.ParticleGroups.sum_values"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#I/O","page":"I/O","text":"Writers and trajectory helpers are kept at the top level so user scripts can write diagnostics without importing internal modules.","title":"I/O"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#Public-writer-API","page":"I/O","text":"","title":"Public writer API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#GSD-metadata-types","page":"I/O","text":"","title":"GSD metadata types"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#Notes","page":"I/O","text":"XYZ and CSV helpers stage data to host for output.\nGSD writing stays outside the simulation hot path and is the preferred trajectory format for richer restart data.","title":"Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/io/#Example-scripts","page":"I/O","text":"examples/3D_quicktest.jl\nexamples/2D_allpairs_quicktest.jl\n\nThese remain GPU-first examples. The small quicktests are the best future CPU-compatible candidates once a CPU backend becomes real.","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.InMemoryLogger","page":"I/O","text":"Lightweight logger that records selected observables in host memory every every steps. Useful for quick ad-hoc diagnostics during testing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.InMemoryLogger"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.CSVWriter","page":"I/O","text":"Stream particle tables to CSV (one row per particle). Mirrors the logging used in the earlier examples/ scripts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.CSVWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.XYZWriter","page":"I/O","text":"Minimal XYZ trajectory writer. Each call to write! appends one frame; z is set to zero for 2D states, matching the usage in examples/2D_example.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.XYZWriter"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_xyz!","page":"I/O","text":"write_xyz!(path; rx, ry[, rz], atomsym=\"A\")\n\nWrite a single XYZ frame without constructing an XYZWriter. Mirrors the ad-hoc dumping performed in examples/2D_example.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_xyz!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_observables_csv!","page":"I/O","text":"write_observables_csv!(path, step; Epot, Ekin, dq)\n\nAppend a single line with step, Etot, Kavg, Qtot (mirrors examples/2D_example.jl and the 3D variants).\n\n\n\n\n\nwrite_observables_csv!(path, st, spec)\n\nWrite observables collected through SimulationCore.collect_step_observables, including a compatibility Qtot column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_observables_csv!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.gsd_open","page":"I/O","text":"gsd_open(f::Function, path; kwargs...)\n\nConvenience do-block form that guarantees the GSD handle is closed, even if an exception occurs during writing. Example:\n\nWriters.gsd_open(\"traj.gsd\") do h\n    for step in 1:ns\n        Writers.write_gsd_frame!(h, st; step)\n    end\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.gsd_open"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.gsd_close","page":"I/O","text":"Close a previously opened GSD handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.gsd_close"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_gsd_frame!","page":"I/O","text":"write_gsd_frame!(h, st; diameter=nothing, mass=nothing, charge=nothing,\n                  types_names=[\"A\"], step=0,\n                  write_forces=false, write_unwrapped=false,\n                  write_virial=false, sync_on_write=false)\n\nWrite one frame of st to the open GSD handle h at the given step.\n\nPositions, and (unless the last integrator step was position-only) velocities, are always written. diameter, mass, and charge accept nothing (use GSD defaults), a scalar, or a per-particle-or-per-type vector (expanded from per-type values via types_names). Set write_forces, write_unwrapped, or write_virial to additionally store forces, unwrapped positions (requires unwrapped_positions=true in build_simulation), or the virial tensor. Output is written in build-time particle order regardless of any internal spatial reordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.write_gsd_frame!"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDFrameData","page":"I/O","text":"Container returned by read_gsd_frame!.\n\nBehaves like the legacy tuple (step, rx, ry, rz, vx, vy, vz, typeid, types, box, force) when destructured/iterated, while exposing richer metadata via fields:\n\nstep, N, D, box: configuration metadata.\nrx, ry, rz, vx, vy, vz: particle state vectors (host arrays).\ntypeid, types: 1-based ids and type names.\nforceM: optional force matrix (N×3, same precision as positions).\nparticle_properties: dictionary with optional particle properties (mass, charge, diameter, body, orientation, etc) converted to sensible Julia arrays. When present, custom force/virial chunks are also exposed as :force and :virial; other particles/property/* chunks appear under the :property key.\nper_type_properties: dictionary for particles/type_* chunks (e.g. :shapes for per-type shape JSON blobs).\ntopology: a GSDTopology with bonds/angles/… converted to 1-based indices.\nconfiguration: hierarchical NamedTuple combining metadata, particle data, topology and any extra configuration chunks for convenience.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDFrameData"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/io/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDTopology","page":"I/O","text":"Structured toplogical information decoded from a GSD frame.\n\nEach field is a NamedTuple describing the corresponding collection and contains both the original 0-based data (group0, typeid0) and the converted 1-based indices (group, typeid) that can be passed directly to ParticleDynamics utilities such as BondedForces.build_bondlist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDTopology"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Integrators","page":"Integrators","text":"The package exposes explicit integrator constructors that allocate and own the workspace needed for repeated stepping. Reuse a spec across many step! calls.","title":"Integrators"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Integrator-constructors","page":"Integrators","text":"","title":"Integrator constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Public-spec-types","page":"Integrators","text":"","title":"Public spec types"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Usage-pattern","page":"Integrators","text":"nve_spec = nve(st; dt=2.0f-4)\nfor _ in 1:100\n    step!(st, nve_spec, 2.0f-4; compute_energy=false)\nend","title":"Usage pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Notes","page":"Integrators","text":"nve is the deterministic microcanonical MD path.\nLangevin and Brownian constructors own stochastic buffers.\ngamma > 0 is required on stochastic paths that divide by friction.\nThermostat-driven MD paths (nosehooverchain, csvr) allocate device workspaces per bath and remain CUDA-specific internally.","title":"Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/integrators/#Example-scripts","page":"Integrators","text":"examples/2D_example.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_BAOAB.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_GSM.jl\nexamples/TwoT_2D_BD_EH.jl\nexamples/3D_BD.jl\nexamples/3D_LJ_NVE.jl","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nve","page":"Integrators","text":"nve(st; dt=st.dt) -> NVESpec\n\nCreate a deterministic microcanonical MD integrator using plain velocity Verlet. Unlike the stochastic velocityverlet constructor, this path owns no thermostat or noise parameters and advances only under conservative forces.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nve"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.velocityverlet","page":"Integrators","text":"velocityverlet(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n               mass=st.mass, dt=st.dt) -> VVSpec\n\nConstruct a GJF/Langevin velocity-Verlet spec with integrator-owned stochastic buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.velocityverlet"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.baoab","page":"Integrators","text":"baoab(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n      mass=st.mass, dt=st.dt) -> BAOABSpec\n\nConstruct a BAOAB Langevin spec with integrator-owned stochastic buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.baoab"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.baoa","page":"Integrators","text":"baoa(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n     mass=st.mass, dt=st.dt) -> BAOASpec\n\nLegacy BAOA variant (no final B kick).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.baoa"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.gsm","page":"Integrators","text":"gsm(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n    mass=st.mass, dt=st.dt) -> GSMSpec\n\nConstruct a GSM spec (used by examples/TwoT_2D_LD_GSM.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.gsm"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.eulerheun","page":"Integrators","text":"eulerheun(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n          dt=st.dt) -> BrownianSpec\n\nBuild a midpoint Brownian spec with integrator-owned stochastic buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.eulerheun"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.eulermaruyama","page":"Integrators","text":"eulermaruyama(st; gamma, temperature, noise_corr_time=nothing, ou_scales=nothing,\n              dt=st.dt) -> EMSpec\n\nReturn an Euler-Maruyama spec for overdamped dynamics (examples/3D_BD.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.eulermaruyama"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nosehooverchain","page":"Integrators","text":"nosehooverchain(st; temperature=1, tau=1, chain_length=5, substeps=5,\n                mass=st.mass, chain_masses=nothing, propagator=:gromacs) -> NHCSpec\n\nCreate a deterministic NVT integrator specification using a Nose-Hoover Chain. When chain_masses is omitted, masses are initialized from (dof, T, tau) using the standard Q₁ = g T τ², Qⱼ = T τ² (j>1) rule. propagator selects the chain update scheme: :gromacs is the package default and uses a GPU port of the reversible Suzuki-Yoshida propagator used by GROMACS, :lammps uses a GPU port of the reversible tloop chain update used by LAMMPS, and :legacy preserves the original package implementation. For propagator=:gromacs, substeps=5 matches the default fifth-order outer repetition used there; for propagator=:lammps, substeps corresponds to LAMMPS tloop and substeps=1 matches the LAMMPS default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.nosehooverchain"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.csvr","page":"Integrators","text":"csvr(st; temperature=1, tau=1, mass=st.mass) -> CSVRSpec\ncsvr(st; temperatures, taus, mass=st.mass) -> CSVRSpec\n\nCreate a deterministic MD integrator using the Bussi canonical-sampling through velocity rescaling (CSVR) thermostat. The thermostat acts on one or more baths defined by filter assignments, with one global velocity-rescaling factor drawn per bath and per timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.csvr"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.IntegratorInterfaces.AbstractIntegratorSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"AbstractIntegratorSpec{T}\n\nBase protocol type for stage-driven time integrators. Concrete specs (VV, BAOAB, Brownian, EM, NHC) should subtype this and provide protocol methods used by the shared stepping engine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.IntegratorInterfaces.AbstractIntegratorSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.VVSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"Concrete spec for the package's Langevin/GJF velocityverlet path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.VVSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BAOABSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"BAOAB splitting spec used in examples/TwoT_2D_LD_BAOAB.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BAOABSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BAOASpec","page":"Integrators","text":"BAOA splitting (no trailing B) for legacy scripts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BAOASpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.GSMSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"Generalized simulation scheme (GSM) spec, which reuses the BAOAB parameter type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.GSMSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BrownianSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"Midpoint Brownian integrator spec created by eulerheun.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.BrownianSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.EMSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"Euler-Maruyama overdamped spec created by eulermaruyama.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.EMSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.NVESpec","page":"Integrators","text":"NVESpec{T}\n\nConcrete integrator specification for deterministic microcanonical (NVE) dynamics using plain velocity Verlet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.NVESpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.NHCSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"NHCSpec{T}\n\nConcrete integrator specification for NVT dynamics with a Nose-Hoover Chain thermostat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.NHCSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/integrators/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.CSVRSpec","page":"Integrators","text":"CSVRSpec{T}\n\nConcrete integrator specification for deterministic NVT dynamics with a stochastic global Bussi/CSVR thermostat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.CSVRSpec"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/thermostats/#Thermostats","page":"Thermostats","text":"Thermostat objects describe bath structure and expose state/parameter accessors that can be used from diagnostics or higher-level workflows.","title":"Thermostats"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/thermostats/#Public-thermostat-API","page":"Thermostats","text":"","title":"Public thermostat API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/thermostats/#Runtime-construction","page":"Thermostats","text":"Thermostat-backed integrator specs are built through:\n\nnosehooverchain(st; ...)\ncsvr(st; ...)\n\nThose constructors remain the recommended entrypoints for stepping.","title":"Runtime construction"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/thermostats/#Example-scripts","page":"Thermostats","text":"examples/TwoT_SR2D_MD_CSVR_slab.jl\nexamples/SingleT_2D_MD_CSVR.jl\nexamples/TwoT_LJ2D_MD_NHC.jl\n\nThe thermostat examples remain GPU-first. Bath-control logic is a likely later candidate for CPU-compatible validation only after kernel portability work.","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.AbstractThermostat","page":"Thermostats","text":"Abstract base type for all thermostat implementations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.AbstractThermostat"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.ThermostatState","page":"Thermostats","text":"Abstract base for thermostat state containers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.ThermostatState"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.NoseHooverChainThermostat","page":"Thermostats","text":"NoseHooverChainThermostat{T} <: AbstractThermostat\n\nDeterministic Nose-Hoover Chain thermostat with multi-bath support.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.NoseHooverChainThermostat"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.CSVRThermostat","page":"Thermostats","text":"CSVRThermostat{T} <: AbstractThermostat\n\nStochastic CSVR/Bussi velocity-rescaling thermostat with multi-bath support.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.CSVRThermostat"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.n_baths","page":"Thermostats","text":"n_baths(thermo::AbstractThermostat) -> Int\n\nNumber of baths in this thermostat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.n_baths"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.target_temperature","page":"Thermostats","text":"target_temperature(thermo::AbstractThermostat) -> Vector\n\nPer-bath target temperatures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.target_temperature"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.response_time","page":"Thermostats","text":"response_time(thermo::AbstractThermostat) -> Vector\n\nPer-bath response times (tau).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.response_time"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.set_target_temperature!","page":"Thermostats","text":"set_target_temperature!(thermo::AbstractThermostat, T_new)\n\nUpdate target temperature(s).\n\nAccepts:\n\nScalar: apply to all baths\nVector: length must match n_baths(thermo)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.set_target_temperature!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.set_response_time!","page":"Thermostats","text":"set_response_time!(thermo::AbstractThermostat, tau_new)\n\nUpdate response time(s).\n\nAccepts:\n\nScalar: apply to all baths\nVector: length must match n_baths(thermo)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.set_response_time!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/thermostats/#ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.cumulative_energy_exchange","page":"Thermostats","text":"cumulative_energy_exchange(thermo::AbstractThermostat) -> CuArray{T}\n\nCumulative energy exchanged with the thermostat reservoir per bath.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Thermostats.cumulative_energy_exchange"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Quickstart","page":"Quickstart","text":"This page shows the recommended public workflow API. It is the interface normal examples and user scripts should use.\n\nParticleDynamics.jl is currently CUDA-first, so the workflow still builds a GPU-resident low-level simulation under the hood. The workflow layer exists to hide manual build_simulation(...), manual stepping loops, manual GSD open/close calls, and manual observable plumbing.","title":"Quickstart"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Conceptual-model","page":"Quickstart","text":"ParticleSystem: positions, box, types, masses, velocities, topology\nGroup / Groups: particle selections\nForce: physical interactions\nIntegrator: dt, scheme, forces, methods, thermostats\nObservable: sampled quantities\nWriter: scheduled output\nStage: a named block of running\nSimulation: assembled workflow object","title":"Conceptual model"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Minimal-workflow","page":"Quickstart","text":"using ParticleDynamics\n\nN = 128\ncfg = hex_random_2d(N, 1.0f0, 0.25f0; T=Float32)\n\nsystem = ParticleSystem(\n    cfg;\n    types=[:A],\n    typeids=fill(Int32(1), N),\n    masses=Dict(:A => 1.0f0),\n)\n\nall = Group(:all, AllSelection())\ngroups = Groups(all)\n\nforce = WCA(\n    epsilon=10.0f0,\n    sigma=1.0f0,\n    cutoff=Float32(2^(1 / 6)),\n    pairs=:all,\n)\n\nintegrator = Integrator(\n    dt=2.0f-4,\n    scheme=VelocityVerlet(),\n    forces=[force],\n    methods=[Langevin(all; gamma=50.0f0, kT=1.0f0)],\n)\n\nthermo = ThermodynamicObservable(all; name=:thermo)\n\nsim = Simulation(\n    system;\n    groups=groups,\n    integrator=integrator,\n    observables=[thermo],\n    writers=[\n        TableWriter(\n            \"obs.csv\";\n            every=50,\n            observables=[thermo => [:temperature, :kinetic_energy, :potential_energy]],\n            mode=:replace,\n        ),\n        GSDWriter(\"traj.gsd\"; every=200, group=all, write_start=true, mode=:replace),\n    ],\n    precision=Float32,\n    seed=0xC9A319,\n)\n\nrun!(sim, Stage(:warmup, steps=200; dt=1.0f-4, neighbor_rebuild_interval=1))\nreset_observables!(sim)\nreset_step!(sim, 0)\nrun!(sim, Stage(:production, steps=1_000))","title":"Minimal workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#NVE-production-after-equilibration","page":"Quickstart","text":"Pure NVE on the workflow API is expressed as ConstantVolume(group) with no thermostat. A common pattern is to equilibrate with a thermostat, then swap the integrator and re-prepare before production:\n\nall = Group(:all, AllSelection())\n\nsim = Simulation(\n    system;\n    groups=Groups(all),\n    integrator=Integrator(\n        dt=2.0f-4,\n        forces=[force],\n        methods=[ConstantVolume(all; thermostat=CSVR(kT=1.0f0, tau=0.1f0))],\n    ),\n)\n\nrun!(sim, Stage(:equil, steps=5_000))\n\nsim.integrator = Integrator(\n    dt=2.0f-4,\n    forces=[force],\n    methods=[ConstantVolume(all)],\n)\nprepare!(sim)\nreset_observables!(sim)\nreset_step!(sim, 0)\nrun!(sim, Stage(:production, steps=20_000))\n\nexamples/3D_LJ_NVE.jl is stricter than this pattern: it runs pure NVE from step 0 and sets the initial velocities explicitly on the ParticleSystem.","title":"NVE production after equilibration"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Groups-apply-methods-and-observables","page":"Quickstart","text":"Groups are currently particle selections. They are designed so the same high-level vocabulary can later grow to bonds, angles, or other topology domains without changing how users think about selection.\n\ncold = Group(:cold, TypeSelection(:C))\nhot = Group(:hot, TypeSelection(:H))\ngroups = Groups(cold, hot, Group(:all, AllSelection()))\n\nMethods and observables attach to those groups:\n\nIntegrator(\n    dt=2.0f-4,\n    scheme=VelocityVerlet(),\n    forces=[force],\n    methods=[\n        Langevin(cold; gamma=50.0f0, kT=5.0f0),\n        Langevin(hot; gamma=50.0f0, kT=100.0f0),\n    ],\n)","title":"Groups apply methods and observables"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Output-is-scheduled,-not-hand-written","page":"Quickstart","text":"Writers own file management and run on schedules:\n\nEvery(n)\nAtSteps([0, 100, 1_000])\nBetween(first, last; every=...)\n\nThis keeps examples free of manual gsd_open, gsd_close, write_gsd_frame!, manual CSV header logic, and direct collect_step_observables(...) calls.","title":"Output is scheduled, not hand-written"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Low-level-expert-API","page":"Quickstart","text":"The older expert API is still supported:\n\nbuild_simulation\nstep!\nstep_graph!\nnve, velocityverlet, baoab, baoa, gsm, eulerheun, eulermaruyama, nosehooverchain, csvr\nParticleDynamics.SimulationCore\n\nUse that surface if you need direct control over the GPU-resident SimulationState, debugging of stepping internals, or custom orchestration that the workflow layer intentionally does not expose.","title":"Low-level expert API"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Current-limitations","page":"Quickstart","text":"CUDA is the main supported backend.\nAngles, dihedrals, impropers, electrostatics, and similar force-field features are future-ready in the vocabulary but not generally implemented.\nForceField is a stable container concept, but compiled support is limited to the existing low-level kernels and wrappers.","title":"Current limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Simulation-State","page":"Simulation State","text":"SimulationState is the long-lived, GPU-resident container returned by build_simulation. It owns particle arrays, neighbor data, observables buffers, and optional restart/output metadata for a single simulation.","title":"Simulation State"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Core-API","page":"Simulation State","text":"","title":"Core API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#What-stays-in-the-state","page":"Simulation State","text":"wrapped positions, velocities, and forces\noptional unwrapped positions\nneighbor-list storage\nbonded and nonbonded parameter storage\nper-particle observables and virial buffers\n\nThe current implementation remains GPU-first: state arrays are CuArray based, and the execution path remains CUDA-specific even though build-time backend selection is now routed through the storage layer.","title":"What stays in the state"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Minimal-pattern","page":"Simulation State","text":"using ParticleDynamics\n\nst = build_simulation(\n    N=64,\n    box=(40.0f0, 40.0f0),\n    cutoff=Float32(2^(1/6)),\n    skin=0.4f0,\n    cap=Int32(32),\n    epsilon=10.0f0,\n    sigma=1.0f0,\n    gamma=50.0f0,\n    temperature=1.0f0,\n    nonbonded=:wca,\n)","title":"Minimal pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/simulation_state/#Example-scripts","page":"Simulation State","text":"examples/2D_allpairs_quicktest.jl: smallest force-path smoke example, and a likely future CPU-compatible smoke candidate once a CPU backend exists.\nexamples/3D_quicktest.jl: small 3D stepping/output example, also a likely future CPU-compatible smoke candidate.\nexamples/TwoT_2D_LD_VV.jl: larger production-style Langevin setup on the workflow API.","title":"Example scripts"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.SimulationState","page":"Simulation State","text":"SimulationState{T}\n\nStructure-of-arrays storage for all GPU-resident buffers required to evolve a non-equilibrium simulation. Users normally do not construct this type manually; build_simulation assembles a fully initialized instance with concrete CuArray fields and consistent dimensionality inferred from the box argument.\n\nKey fields that user code may read or mutate:\n\nrx, ry[, rz], vx, vy[, vz], fx, fy[, fz]: positions, velocities, and force accumulators in GPU memory. Arrays are sized N and remain allocated for the entire simulation so that stepping is allocation-free.\nrx_unwrap, ry_unwrap[, rz_unwrap]: optional unwrapped positions that track continuous motion across periodic boundaries (enabled via unwrapped_positions).\nnbh: neighbor matrix (either dense cell list or sentinel all-pairs) built with the NeighborLists.build_neighbors_* family using the requested cutoff, skin, and capacity.\nmass, dt: nominal build-time timestep plus the scalar fast-path mass used when all particles share one mass. Heterogeneous-mass states additionally populate mass_particle and inv_mass_particle with per-particle data.\nEpot, Ekin, virial, dq, dU plus the corresponding *_accum buffers: energy, virial, and heat observables that can be sampled directly on the GPU.\nvirial_nonbonded, virial_bonded, virial_tensor, virial_tensor_accum: GPU-resident configurational virial tensors. These store the raw pairwise virial W = Σ r_ij ⊗ F_ij using the same minimum-image displacement used in the force kernels. The scalar virial field stores the trace of W for backward compatibility with existing examples. These buffers are refreshed on force evaluations with compute_energy=true.\ntypeid: per-particle type ids used by Filters and mixed-size LJ kernels.\nfreeze_*: optional buffers used by the freeze/tether helpers in Filters.\ncoll_*: optional buffers that appear when collision counting is enabled via enable_collision_counting!.\n\nAll other fields are internal implementation details (neighbor rebuild state, bond lists, cached LJ parameters, etc.) and should be treated as read-only. Current backend abstraction is storage-level only: SimulationState remains CuArray-backed today, and the execution kernels are still CUDA-specific.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.SimulationState"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.build_simulation","page":"Simulation State","text":"build_simulation(; N, box, cutoff=1, skin=0.4, cap=Int32(96),\n                  neigh_interval=20, use_neighborlist=true,\n                  epsilon=1, sigma=1, gamma=1, temperature=1,\n                  noise_corr_time=nothing, dt=0.001,\n                  mass=1, bonds=nothing, bonding=nothing,\n                  nonbonded=:lj, softrep_params=nothing, backend=:cuda,\n                  precision=:f32, unwrapped_positions::Bool=false,\n                  spatial_reorder::Bool=true, reorder_interval::Int=500)\n\nConstruct a SimulationState with GPU-resident SoA arrays and a neighbor list configured for the requested potential. All inputs are keyword arguments so that scripts can copy known-good parameter sets directly from the examples/ directory without ambiguity.\n\nKey behaviors:\n\nThe simulation dimensionality (2D vs 3D) is inferred from the length of box. Positions/velocities/forces allocate the corresponding CuArrays.\nFor nonbonded = :wca the neighbor cutoff is forced to the physical WCA value r_c = 2^(1/6) σ even if a larger cutoff was passed, guaranteeing that kernels reuse the validated parameter sets from the packaged examples.\ntemperature accepts either a scalar or length-N vector and is used to initialize velocities. Stochastic integrator parameters are constructed later via explicit specs such as velocityverlet and eulermaruyama.\nmass accepts either a scalar or a length-N vector. Initial velocities are drawn from a Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution with width sqrt(kT / m) for particles with positive mass, then centered to remove center-of-mass drift.\nWhen unwrapped_positions=true, additional rx_unwrap/ry_unwrap/rz_unwrap buffers track continuous positions across periodic boundaries.\nWith spatial_reorder=true (the default) and a dense cell list, NVE runs periodically permute all per-particle arrays into cell-sorted order (at most once per reorder_interval steps, at a neighbor rebuild). This keeps storage order aligned with space, which is essential for force-kernel memory locality at large N. st.tag[k] records the build-time id of the particle in slot k, and GSD/restart output is written in build-time order. Pass spatial_reorder=false if external code indexes into the state arrays by fixed particle number (e.g. Filters.Indices); typeid-based selections remain valid because typeid is permuted consistently.\n\nExample (mirrors examples/2D_example.jl, scaled down to N=4096 for testing):\n\nst = build_simulation(N=4096, box=(250f0, 250f0),\n                      cutoff=Float32(2^(1/6)), skin=Float32(2^(1/6))/2,\n                      cap=Int32(250), neigh_interval=50,\n                      epsilon=1f4, sigma=1f0,\n                      gamma=615f0, temperature=10f0,\n                      dt=1f-5, nonbonded=:wca, precision=:f32)\nvv = velocityverlet(st; gamma=615f0, temperature=10f0, dt=1f-5)\nstep!(st, vv, 1f-5; compute_energy=false)\n\nReturns a fully initialized SimulationState. Stochastic controls such as noise_corr_time now belong to the explicit integrator spec rather than the core simulation state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.build_simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.sync_unwrapped!","page":"Simulation State","text":"sync_unwrapped!(st)\n\nCopy the wrapped positions into the unwrapped buffers. Call this after manually overwriting st.rx/st.ry/st.rz when unwrapped_positions=true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.sync_unwrapped!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.step!","page":"Simulation State","text":"step!(st, spec, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCanonical explicit stepping API.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, vv, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit VVParams stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, bao, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit BAOABParams stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, bp, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Brownian midpoint stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, em, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Euler-Maruyama stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, nhc, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Nose-Hoover Chain parameter stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep!(st, csvr_params, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit CSVR parameter stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.step!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/simulation_state/#ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.step_graph!","page":"Simulation State","text":"step_graph!(st, spec, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nGraph-entry stepping API with explicit integrator selection. It currently shares the same stage-driven engine as step!.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, vv, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit VVParams graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, bao, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit BAOABParams graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, bp, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Brownian midpoint graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, em, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Euler-Maruyama graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, nhc, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit Nose-Hoover chain graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_graph!(st, csvr_params, dt; compute_energy=true)\n\nCompatibility wrapper for explicit CSVR graph stepping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.step_graph!"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/collision_rate/#Collision-Rate-Notes-(Legacy)","page":"Collision Rate Notes","text":"The historical collision-rate derivation note remains available at:\n\ndocs/CollisionRate.md\n\nThis page is kept as a pointer while the new manual collision documentation is being consolidated.","title":"Collision Rate Notes (Legacy)"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#Restarts","page":"Restarts","text":"Restart support currently centers on reading GSD frames back into a structured host-side container that can be used to rebuild a simulation.","title":"Restarts"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#Public-restart-API","page":"Restarts","text":"","title":"Public restart API"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#What-read_gsd_frame!-returns","page":"Restarts","text":"read_gsd_frame! returns a GSDFrameData object that:\n\npreserves legacy tuple-style destructuring for older scripts\nexposes particle arrays, topology, and metadata through named fields\nkeeps restart parsing outside the hot CUDA stepping path\n\nThe metadata container types themselves are documented on the I/O page:\n\nParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDFrameData\nParticleDynamics.Writers.GSDTopology","title":"What read_gsd_frame! returns"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#Minimal-pattern","page":"Restarts","text":"frame = read_gsd_frame!(\"traj.gsd\")\n@show frame.step frame.N frame.box","title":"Minimal pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/restarts/#Related-scripts","page":"Restarts","text":"examples/3D_quicktest.jl for write-side GSD usage\nexamples/restart_from_gsd.jl for the high-level restart workflow wrapper\nrestart-oriented user workflows should treat current examples as GPU-first; later CPU-compatible restart smoke tests can be added after a CPU backend exists","title":"Related scripts"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/restarts/#ParticleDynamics.Writers.read_gsd_frame!","page":"Restarts","text":"Read a valid frame from a GSD file and return a GSDFrameData.\n\nThe returned object can still be destructured into the legacy tuple step, rx, ry, rz, vx, vy, vz, typeid, types, box, force for backwards compatibility, while also exposing rich metadata (frame.topology, frame.particle_properties, frame.configuration, …) that can be fed directly into ParticleDynamics initialisation routines.\n\nArguments:\n\nfile_path: path to the GSD file.\nstep: optional timestep or frame index to target. When nothing, the most recent complete frame is returned. When provided, the routine first searches for a frame whose configuration/step matches and, if none exist, treats the value as a 1-based frame index.\n\nThrows:\n\nArgumentError if mandatory chunks are missing or no readable frame exists.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ParticleDynamics.Writers.read_gsd_frame!"},{"category":"section","location":"#ParticleDynamics.jl","page":"Home","text":"ParticleDynamics.jl is a GPU-first Julia package for non-equilibrium particle simulations on CUDA.jl. The recommended public surface is the workflow API: ParticleSystem, Group, Force, Integrator, Observable, Writer, Stage, Simulation, and run!.\n\n<div style=\"padding:0.6rem 0.8rem; border-left:4px solid #d9534f; background:#fff6f6;\">\n<strong>GPU-only package:</strong> a functional CUDA environment is required for simulations and test-validated behavior.\n</div>","title":"ParticleDynamics.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Scope","page":"Home","text":"2D/3D particle dynamics with periodic boundaries.\nDeterministic MD: pure NVE plus thermostat-driven constant-volume dynamics.\nLangevin integrators: velocity-Verlet (default), BAOAB, BAOA, GSM.\nBrownian integrators: midpoint (Euler-Heun style) and Euler-Maruyama.\nNonbonded interactions (LJ/WCA/soft-repulsive), bonded interactions (harmonic/FENE), filters/freeze controls, collision counting, and XYZ/CSV/GSD outputs.","title":"Scope"},{"category":"section","location":"#Workflow-model","page":"Home","text":"ParticleSystem stores positions, types, masses, box, velocities, and topology.\nGroup selects particles today and is designed to grow into other topology domains later.\nForce objects describe physical interactions.\nIntegrator owns dt, forces, methods, and thermostats.\nObservable objects compute sampled quantities.\nWriter objects own scheduled output.\nStage describes a named block of running.\nSimulation assembles the workflow, and run! owns the timestep loop.","title":"Workflow model"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Links","page":"Home","text":"Workflow start: Quickstart\nExpert API: Low-Level API\nState layout: Simulation State\nIntegrators: Integrators\nInteractions: Forces\nSelection and controls: Groups and Filters\nThermostat interfaces: Thermostats\nDiagnostics: Observables\nOutput formats: I/O\nRestart reading: Restarts\nLegacy notes: Collision Rate Notes\nExample inventory: examples/README.md","title":"Quick Links"},{"category":"section","location":"#Public-API-map","page":"Home","text":"The supported top-level API is intentionally split into a workflow layer and a low-level expert layer.\n\nAPI group Exported symbols Primary page\nWorkflow Simulation, ParticleSystem, Topology, Group, Groups, Force objects, Integrator, Observable objects, Writer objects, Stage, prepare!, run!, reset_step!, reset_observables!, state quickstart.md\nLow-level expert API SimulationState, build_simulation, step!, step_graph!, sync_unwrapped!, collect_step_observables, reset_bath_exchange_accumulators!, nve, velocityverlet, baoab, baoa, gsm, eulerheun, eulermaruyama, nosehooverchain, csvr manual/getting_started.md, manual/simulation_state.md, manual/integrators.md\nParameters and physical helpers LJParams, SoftRepulsiveParams, HarmonicBondParams, FENEParams, BondPotential, HarmonicBond, FENEBond, harmonic_bond, fene_bond, StokesFrictionCoefficient, SphereMass, InertialTime, DiffusiveTime manual/forces.md\nInitialization generators box_from_phi_2d, box_from_phi_3d, hex_random_2d, hex_circle_2d, hex_circle_plus_random_2d, hex_sites_in_box_2d, hex_circle_in_box_2d, hex_slab_coexistence_2d, fcc_sites_in_box_3d, fcc_random_3d, fcc_slab_coexistence_3d manual/getting_started.md\nWriters and I/O write_xyz!, write_observables_csv!, gsd_open, gsd_close, write_gsd_frame!, read_gsd_frame!, InMemoryLogger, CSVWriter, XYZWriter manual/io.md\nBond lists BondList, build_bondlist manual/forces.md\nCollision counting enable_collision_counting!, disable_collision_counting!, collisions_reset_counts!, collisions_read_counts!, set_collision_pair_cutoffs! manual/observables.md\nSelection helpers and modules ParticleSelection, ParticleGroup, All, TypeIDs, Indices, materialize, apply_scalar!, apply_values!, gather, sum_values, plus the exported Filters/BondedForces modules for qualified access manual/groups_filters.md, manual/forces.md\n\nAdvanced helpers that are intentionally not part of the default import surface live under ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore, for example ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.zero_forces!, ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.accumulate_energies!, ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.run_integrator_step!, ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.thermostatted_dof, and ParticleDynamics.SimulationCore.thermostatted_particle_mask.","title":"Public API map"},{"category":"section","location":"#Behavior-Baseline-(from-tests)","page":"Home","text":"Current tests validate:\n\nDeterministic force/kernel properties (analytic 2-particle checks, Newton symmetry, PBC invariance, backend parity).\nStochastic behavior at moment level (Brownian MSD slope, Langevin equipartition, OU trend checks, weak-step trend in deterministic limit).\nRegression/IR fixes including gamma > 0 enforced error behavior on stochastic paths where required.","title":"Behavior Baseline (from tests)"},{"category":"section","location":"#Current-limitations","page":"Home","text":"CUDA is the primary supported backend.\nAngles, dihedrals, impropers, electrostatics, and broader force-field concepts are future-facing vocabulary unless already implemented in the existing low-level engine.\nForceField is stable as a container concept, but compiled support remains limited to the force families backed by current kernels and wrappers.","title":"Current limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"#Build-docs-locally","page":"Home","text":"julia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate(); include(\"docs/make.jl\")'","title":"Build docs locally"}]
}
